            <ArticleTitle>Effects of Myricetin-Containing Ethanol Solution on High-Fat Diet Induced Obese Rats.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1947-1952</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/1750-3841.13755</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Myricetin is a natural flavonol widely occurring in wines. Many beneficial effects of myricetin in alcoholic beverages have been reported before, but never including anti-obesity. In the present study, we fed obese male Sprague-Dawley rats with ethanol solutions containing various concentrations of myricetin and found that myricetin may maintain the food intake while reduce the weight-gain, feed efficiency, level of blood lipids, adipocyte size, and weight and size of the perirenal and epididymal adipose tissues (P &lt; 0.01). Our experiment data also show that the anti-obesity effect may be associated with the upregulation of adropin and β-endorphin levels. Based on the above-described findings, we propose the potential for myricetin-containing alcoholic beverages to be developed into anti-obesity health food.</AbstractText>
